 "answer": "The pgvector `<#>` operator returns the **negative inner product** between two vectors, as a `double precision` value.\n\nIt's negated so that `ORDER BY embedding <#> query` ascending returns the most similar rows first, since a larger inner product means more similar. To get the actual inner product, multiply the result by -1, for example `(a <#> b) * -1`.\n\nThis is the standard pgvector behavior as I know it. I don't have information about how your particular project uses it.",
